1
00:00:05,519 --> 00:00:09,277
My hands had been dressed twice or thrice in the night, and again in

2
00:00:09,277 --> 00:00:14,635
the morning. My left arm was a good deal burned to the elbow, and, less severely,

3
00:00:14,637 --> 00:00:20,398
as high as the shoulder; it was very painful, but the flames had set in that direction,

4
00:00:20,398 --> 00:00:26,917
and I felt thankful it was no worse. My right hand was not so badly burnt but that I could move the

5
00:00:26,917 --> 00:00:34,319
fingers. It was bandaged, of course, but much less inconveniently than my left hand and arm;

6
00:00:34,319 --> 00:00:40,839
those I carried in a sling; and I could only wear my coat like a cloak, loose over my shoulders

7
00:00:40,839 --> 00:00:48,559
and fastened at the neck. My hair had been caught by the fire, but not my head or face.

8
00:00:48,559 --> 00:00:54,758
When Herbert had been down to Hammersmith and seen his father, he came back to me at our chambers,

9
00:00:54,758 --> 00:01:00,879
and devoted the day to attending on me. He was the kindest of nurses, and at stated

10
00:01:00,879 --> 00:01:06,237
times took off the bandages, and steeped them in the cooling liquid that was kept ready,

11
00:01:06,237 --> 00:01:11,678
and put them on again, with a patient tenderness that I was deeply grateful for.

12
00:01:11,680 --> 00:01:19,358
At first, as I lay quiet on the sofa, I found it painfully difficult, I might say impossible,

13
00:01:19,358 --> 00:01:24,519
to get rid of the impression of the glare of the flames, their hurry and noise,

14
00:01:24,519 --> 00:01:32,519
and the fierce burning smell. If I dozed for a minute, I was awakened by Miss Havisham’s cries,

15
00:01:32,519 --> 00:01:37,597
and by her running at me with all that height of fire above her head. This pain

16
00:01:37,597 --> 00:01:42,559
of the mind was much harder to strive against than any bodily pain I suffered;

17
00:01:42,559 --> 00:01:47,680
and Herbert, seeing that, did his utmost to hold my attention engaged.

18
00:01:47,680 --> 00:01:52,319
Neither of us spoke of the boat, but we both thought of it. That was made apparent by our

19
00:01:52,319 --> 00:01:57,160
avoidance of the subject, and by our agreeing—without agreement—to make my

20
00:01:57,160 --> 00:02:03,957
recovery of the use of my hands a question of so many hours, not of so many weeks.

21
00:02:03,957 --> 00:02:10,358
My first question when I saw Herbert had been of course, whether all was well down the river?

22
00:02:10,358 --> 00:02:15,917
As he replied in the affirmative, with perfect confidence and cheerfulness, we did not resume

23
00:02:15,917 --> 00:02:22,116
the subject until the day was wearing away. But then, as Herbert changed the bandages,

24
00:02:22,116 --> 00:02:28,199
more by the light of the fire than by the outer light, he went back to it spontaneously.

25
00:02:28,199 --> 00:02:32,879
“I sat with Provis last night, Handel, two good hours.”

26
00:02:32,879 --> 00:02:33,839
“Where was Clara?”

27
00:02:34,796 --> 00:02:39,078
“Dear little thing!” said Herbert. “She was up and down with Gruffandgrim all

28
00:02:39,078 --> 00:02:42,436
the evening. He was perpetually pegging at the floor the moment

29
00:02:42,438 --> 00:02:46,796
she left his sight. I doubt if he can hold out long, though. What with rum

30
00:02:46,796 --> 00:02:51,680
and pepper,—and pepper and rum,—I should think his pegging must be nearly over.”

31
00:02:51,680 --> 00:02:54,078
“And then you will be married, Herbert?”

32
00:02:54,078 --> 00:02:58,398
“How can I take care of the dear child otherwise?—Lay your arm out upon the back of

33
00:02:58,398 --> 00:03:03,319
the sofa, my dear boy, and I’ll sit down here, and get the bandage off so gradually that you

34
00:03:03,319 --> 00:03:10,957
shall not know when it comes. I was speaking of Provis. Do you know, Handel, he improves?”

35
00:03:10,957 --> 00:03:14,319
“I said to you I thought he was softened when I last saw him.”

36
00:03:14,319 --> 00:03:20,758
“So you did. And so he is. He was very communicative last night,

37
00:03:20,758 --> 00:03:24,637
and told me more of his life. You remember his breaking off

38
00:03:24,637 --> 00:03:30,078
here about some woman that he had had great trouble with.—Did I hurt you?”

39
00:03:30,078 --> 00:03:36,116
I had started, but not under his touch. His words had given me a start.

40
00:03:36,116 --> 00:03:41,398
“I had forgotten that, Herbert, but I remember it now you speak of it.”

41
00:03:41,398 --> 00:03:44,398
“Well! He went into that part of his life,

42
00:03:44,398 --> 00:03:51,678
and a dark wild part it is. Shall I tell you? Or would it worry you just now?”

43
00:03:51,680 --> 00:03:54,637
“Tell me by all means. Every word.”

44
00:03:54,637 --> 00:03:59,718
Herbert bent forward to look at me more nearly, as if my reply had been rather more hurried or

45
00:03:59,718 --> 00:04:05,559
more eager than he could quite account for. “Your head is cool?” he said, touching it.

46
00:04:05,559 --> 00:04:10,519
“Quite,” said I. “Tell me what Provis said, my dear Herbert.”

47
00:04:10,519 --> 00:04:15,038
“It seems,” said Herbert, “—there’s a bandage off most charmingly,

48
00:04:15,038 --> 00:04:19,480
and now comes the cool one,—makes you shrink at first, my poor dear fellow,

49
00:04:19,480 --> 00:04:25,957
don’t it? but it will be comfortable presently,—it seems that the woman was a young woman,

50
00:04:25,957 --> 00:04:33,319
and a jealous woman, and a revengeful woman; revengeful, Handel, to the last degree.”

51
00:04:33,319 --> 00:04:35,557
“To what last degree?”

52
00:04:35,559 --> 00:04:40,438
“Murder.—Does it strike too cold on that sensitive place?”

53
00:04:40,438 --> 00:04:45,199
“I don’t feel it. How did she murder? Whom did she murder?”

54
00:04:45,199 --> 00:04:49,398
“Why, the deed may not have merited quite so terrible a name,” said Herbert, “but,

55
00:04:49,398 --> 00:04:55,038
she was tried for it, and Mr. Jaggers defended her, and the reputation of that defence first

56
00:04:55,038 --> 00:05:00,436
made his name known to Provis. It was another and a stronger woman who was the victim,

57
00:05:00,438 --> 00:05:07,839
and there had been a struggle—in a barn. Who began it, or how fair it was, or how unfair,

58
00:05:07,839 --> 00:05:15,277
may be doubtful; but how it ended is certainly not doubtful, for the victim was found throttled.”

59
00:05:15,277 --> 00:05:17,436
“Was the woman brought in guilty?”

60
00:05:17,438 --> 00:05:20,839
“No; she was acquitted.—My poor Handel, I hurt you!”

61
00:05:20,839 --> 00:05:26,519
“It is impossible to be gentler, Herbert. Yes? What else?”

62
00:05:26,519 --> 00:05:32,038
“This acquitted young woman and Provis had a little child; a little child of whom Provis

63
00:05:32,038 --> 00:05:37,637
was exceedingly fond. On the evening of the very night when the object of her jealousy

64
00:05:37,637 --> 00:05:43,277
was strangled as I tell you, the young woman presented herself before Provis for one moment,

65
00:05:43,277 --> 00:05:48,319
and swore that she would destroy the child (which was in her possession), and he should never see

66
00:05:48,319 --> 00:05:55,597
it again; then she vanished.—There’s the worst arm comfortably in the sling once more, and now there

67
00:05:55,597 --> 00:06:02,398
remains but the right hand, which is a far easier job. I can do it better by this light than by a

68
00:06:02,398 --> 00:06:08,678
stronger, for my hand is steadiest when I don’t see the poor blistered patches too distinctly.—You

69
00:06:08,680 --> 00:06:14,160
don’t think your breathing is affected, my dear boy? You seem to breathe quickly.”

70
00:06:14,160 --> 00:06:19,078
“Perhaps I do, Herbert. Did the woman keep her oath?”

71
00:06:19,078 --> 00:06:23,160
“There comes the darkest part of Provis’s life. She did.”

72
00:06:23,160 --> 00:06:26,237
“That is, he says she did.”

73
00:06:26,237 --> 00:06:30,718
“Why, of course, my dear boy,” returned Herbert, in a tone of surprise, and again

74
00:06:30,718 --> 00:06:36,879
bending forward to get a nearer look at me. “He says it all. I have no other information.”

75
00:06:36,879 --> 00:06:39,000
“No, to be sure.”

76
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:45,078
“Now, whether,” pursued Herbert, “he had used the child’s mother ill, or whether he had used

77
00:06:45,078 --> 00:06:51,000
the child’s mother well, Provis doesn’t say; but she had shared some four or five years

78
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:56,957
of the wretched life he described to us at this fireside, and he seems to have felt pity for her,

79
00:06:56,957 --> 00:07:03,038
and forbearance towards her. Therefore, fearing he should be called upon to depose about this

80
00:07:03,038 --> 00:07:09,957
destroyed child, and so be the cause of her death, he hid himself (much as he grieved for the child),

81
00:07:09,957 --> 00:07:13,917
kept himself dark, as he says, out of the way and out of the trial,

82
00:07:13,917 --> 00:07:18,839
and was only vaguely talked of as a certain man called Abel, out of whom the jealousy

83
00:07:18,839 --> 00:07:26,519
arose. After the acquittal she disappeared, and thus he lost the child and the child’s mother.”

84
00:07:26,519 --> 00:07:28,358
“I want to ask—”

85
00:07:28,358 --> 00:07:35,519
“A moment, my dear boy, and I have done. That evil genius, Compeyson, the worst of scoundrels among

86
00:07:35,519 --> 00:07:41,879
many scoundrels, knowing of his keeping out of the way at that time and of his reasons for doing so,

87
00:07:41,879 --> 00:07:47,160
of course afterwards held the knowledge over his head as a means of keeping him poorer and

88
00:07:47,160 --> 00:07:54,879
working him harder. It was clear last night that this barbed the point of Provis’s animosity.”

89
00:07:54,879 --> 00:08:00,718
“I want to know,” said I, “and particularly, Herbert, whether he told you when this happened?”

90
00:08:00,718 --> 00:08:06,957
“Particularly? Let me remember, then, what he said as to that. His expression was,

91
00:08:06,957 --> 00:08:11,277
‘a round score o’ year ago, and a’most directly after I took up

92
00:08:11,277 --> 00:08:16,480
wi’ Compeyson.’ How old were you when you came upon him in the little churchyard?”

93
00:08:16,480 --> 00:08:18,718
“I think in my seventh year.”

94
00:08:18,718 --> 00:08:23,038
“Ay. It had happened some three or four years then, he said, and you brought

95
00:08:23,038 --> 00:08:29,000
into his mind the little girl so tragically lost, who would have been about your age.”

96
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:33,637
“Herbert,” said I, after a short silence, in a hurried way,

97
00:08:33,637 --> 00:08:38,038
“can you see me best by the light of the window, or the light of the fire?”

98
00:08:38,038 --> 00:08:41,680
“By the firelight,” answered Herbert, coming close again.

99
00:08:41,680 --> 00:08:43,199
“Look at me.”

100
00:08:43,199 --> 00:08:45,718
“I do look at you, my dear boy.”

101
00:08:45,718 --> 00:08:47,000
“Touch me.”

102
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:49,597
“I do touch you, my dear boy.”

103
00:08:49,597 --> 00:08:51,839
“You are not afraid that I am in any fever,

104
00:08:51,839 --> 00:08:56,319
or that my head is much disordered by the accident of last night?”

105
00:08:56,319 --> 00:08:59,038
“N-no, my dear boy,” said Herbert,

106
00:08:59,038 --> 00:09:03,436
after taking time to examine me. “You are rather excited, but you are quite yourself.”

107
00:09:04,680 --> 00:09:08,680
“I know I am quite myself. And the man we have in hiding down the river,

108
00:09:08,680 --> 00:09:10,991
is Estella’s Father.” Chapter 51.
